<!-- ============================================================
     EtherRAT Detection Rules
     Threat: EtherRAT Node.js RAT delivered via Microsoft Teams Vishing
     Source: Palo Alto Unit 42 / BleepingComputer, July 2026
     MITRE ATT&CK Techniques:
       T1566.004 - Phishing: Voice Calls via Teams (TA0001)
       T1219     - Remote Access Software / Quick Assist (TA0011)
       T1059.006 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Node.js (TA0002)
       T1036.005 - Masquerading: Match Legitimate Name/Location (TA0005)
       T1547.001 - Boot/Logon Autostart: Registry Run Keys (TA0003)
       T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols / Ethereum C2 (TA0011)
     Rule IDs: 104014-104019
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-19

     Threat Summary:

     EtherRAT is a cross-platform Node.js remote access trojan (RAT)
     delivered through Microsoft Teams vishing campaigns. Threat actors
     impersonate corporate IT support staff during Teams voice calls and
     convince employees to grant remote access (via Quick Assist,
     HopToDesk, or AnyDesk) and execute a downloaded MSI package
     containing EtherRAT.

     Key technical characteristics:
       - Node.js RAT with Ethereum blockchain-based C2 resolution
         (EtherHiding): queries an Ethereum smart contract to resolve the
         active C2 IP, falling back to a conventional domain. Hardcoded
         C2 IPs are never present in the binary.
       - Install directory derived from MD5(COMPUTERNAME+USERNAME);
         folder names drawn from two fixed word lists:
           tier-1: Microsoft, Windows, Programs, Packages, Google
           tier-2: Services, Components, Assemblies, Extensions, Modules
         Result: C:\Users\{user}\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Components\
         or similar legitimate-looking subdirectory.
       - Persistence via HKCU\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\
         CurrentVersion\Run\EdgeUpdate, masquerading as the Microsoft
         Edge auto-updater (MicrosoftEdgeUpdate.exe).
       - Persistence command proxies node.exe through conhost.exe with
         the undocumented - -headless flag to suppress the console window
         and blend with Windows process tree activity.
       - Debug log written to %APPDATA%\svchost.log, masquerading as a
         Windows service process artifact.
       - Checks for existing Node.js installation via "where node"; if
         absent, downloads node.exe from official nodejs.org CDN using
         curl -sLo, staging it in the derived LOCALAPPDATA directory.
       - Multiple installer versions (v1-v9) found on open distribution
         server; campaign is under active development as of July 2026.

     Attack chain:
       1. Phishing email with "Employee Survey" PDF lure sent to target
       2. Teams voice call from external attacker account posing as IT
       3. Victim grants remote control via Quick Assist / HopToDesk /
          AnyDesk at attacker instruction
       4. Attacker drops and executes EtherRAT MSI through remote session
       5. MSI extracts loader to random LOCALAPPDATA subdirectory
       6. Loader queries Ethereum smart contract for active C2 server
       7. HKCU Run\EdgeUpdate persistence established for user autostart
       8. node.exe executed via conhost.exe - -headless to mask RAT in
          process trees and suppress console window

     Affected platforms: Windows (primary); Linux and macOS variants
     have been observed in the wild with adapted persistence mechanisms.

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
         Required for rules 104014, 104015, 104016, 104018.
         Sysmon must be configured to capture CommandLine and
         ParentImage fields (default in most Sysmon configs).
       - Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644
         Required for rule 104019 (svchost.log artifact).
         Sysmon TargetFilename filter should include *.log patterns
         in user APPDATA paths, or use a broad exclude-only filter.
       - Sysmon EventID 13 (RegistryValueSet) - Wazuh SID 61645
         Required for rule 104017 (EdgeUpdate persistence).
         Sysmon registry monitoring must include:
           HKCU\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run

     Severity tiers:
       11 - Quick Assist/RMM tool spawning shell (context enrichment)
       12 - Teams spawning scripting interpreter or package installer
       12 - svchost.log debug artifact in APPDATA\Roaming (IOC match)
       13 - node.exe executed from LOCALAPPDATA path (anomalous exec)
       13 - conhost.exe - -headless from non-system context (defense evasion)
       14 - HKCU Run EdgeUpdate pointing to non-Edge path (IOC exact match)

     References:
       BleepingComputer 2026-07-06:
         https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/fake-it-support-calls-on-microsoft-teams-push-etherrat-malware/
       Palo Alto Unit 42 2026-06-28:
         https://github.com/PaloAltoNetworks/Unit42-timely-threat-intel/blob/main/2026-06-28-Fake-IT-support-abuses-Teams-to-deliver-EtherRAT.txt
       Malwarebytes Threat Intel 2026-06:
         https://www.malwarebytes.com/blog/threat-intel/2026/06/inside-a-malicious-infrastructure-delivering-etherrat-phishing-pages-and-malicious-software
       eSentire EtherRAT/EtherHiding analysis:
         https://www.esentire.com/blog/etherrat-sys-info-module-c2-on-ethereum-etherhiding-target-selection-cdn-like-beacons
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="execution,initial_access,persistence,etherrat,nodejs_rat,teams_vishing,windows,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104014: Microsoft Teams spawning suspicious child processes
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603

       EtherRAT vishing attacks use a Teams call as the social
       engineering vector. The attacker instructs the victim to run
       actions from within the Teams session. In some variants, Teams
       itself may be the parent of a script launcher or installer if
       the attacker pastes a command into the Teams chat or leverages
       a Teams-integrated workflow to kick off the payload.

       Microsoft Teams (ms-teams.exe / Teams.exe) should never spawn
       scripting interpreters (mshta.exe, wscript.exe, cscript.exe),
       node.exe, or invoke msiexec.exe for arbitrary packages in normal
       enterprise operation. Legitimate Teams subprocess spawns are
       confined to its own browser engine, crash handler, and helper
       processes (msedgewebview2.exe, Teams*.exe subprocesses).

       False positives: Teams automation or bot frameworks that invoke
       PowerShell via Teams connectors may generate similar parent-child
       chains. Tune by adding specific script paths or commandLine
       patterns to a noise suppression rule in your environment after
       establishing a baseline.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104014" level="12">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\ms-teams\.exe$|\\Teams\.exe$)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\node\.exe$|\\mshta\.exe$|\\wscript\.exe$|\\cscript\.exe$|\\msiexec\.exe$)</field>
    <description>EtherRAT/Teams Vishing: Teams process $(win.eventdata.parentImage) spawned suspicious child $(win.eventdata.image) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  EtherRAT vishing uses Teams calls to social-engineer victims into running installers or script engines; investigate for unauthorized remote-session activity or EtherRAT installation (T1566.004, T1059.006)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1566.004</id>
      <id>T1059.006</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>etherrat,teams_vishing,suspicious_child_process,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104015: node.exe executed from LOCALAPPDATA path
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603

       EtherRAT installs a copy of node.exe into a hidden directory
       under %LOCALAPPDATA% derived from MD5(COMPUTERNAME+USERNAME).
       The installer first runs "where node" to find an existing
       installation; if absent, it downloads node.exe from nodejs.org
       via curl and places it in the derived directory, e.g.:
         C:\Users\{user}\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Components\node.exe
         C:\Users\{user}\AppData\Local\Windows\Assemblies\node.exe

       Legitimate Node.js installations on Windows place node.exe at:
         C:\Program Files\nodejs\node.exe
         C:\Program Files (x86)\nodejs\node.exe
       Package managers (nvm-windows, Scoop, Chocolatey, winget) use
       Program Files paths or Scoop-specific App directories, not
       LOCALAPPDATA. nvm for Windows uses APPDATA\Roaming\nvm, not Local.

       node.exe as a standalone executable launched from any path
       under C:\Users\*\AppData\Local\ is strongly anomalous in
       enterprise environments and is a reliable EtherRAT indicator.

       False positives: some portable Node.js dev tool packages may
       unzip to LOCALAPPDATA on developer workstations. Investigate
       before whitelisting; legitimate dev tools do not masquerade
       under Microsoft\ or Windows\ subdirectories.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104015" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)C:\\Users\\[^\\]+\\AppData\\Local\\.*\\node\.exe$</field>
    <description>EtherRAT: node.exe executed from anomalous LOCALAPPDATA path $(win.eventdata.image) by user $(win.eventdata.user) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  EtherRAT installs node.exe under %LOCALAPPDATA%\{MD5-derived-dir}\ using legitimate-sounding folder names (e.g., Microsoft\Components); LOCALAPPDATA node.exe is a strong EtherRAT or similar LOCALAPPDATA-resident Node.js RAT indicator (T1059.006)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.006</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>etherrat,nodejs_rat,anomalous_node_path,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104016: conhost.exe - -headless proxy for node.exe execution
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603

       EtherRAT's persistence mechanism launches node.exe indirectly
       via conhost.exe with the undocumented - -headless argument to
       suppress the console window and obscure the RAT in process trees:

         conhost.exe - -headless cmd.exe /c node.exe %LOCALAPPDATA%\...\main.js

       The - -headless flag is a documented-but-undocumented internal
       Windows argument used by the Console Host to run a child process
       without creating a visible console window. EtherRAT abuses this
       to make the node.exe RAT process appear as a nested conhost child
       rather than a direct user-started process.

       Legitimate system use of conhost.exe - -headless occurs inside
       svchost.exe / services.exe for Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL)
       and some system shell operations. User-context conhost.exe with
       - -headless in the commandLine, especially when not parented by
       svchost, wsl.exe, or services.exe, is anomalous.

       Correlate with rule 104017 (EdgeUpdate Run key) to confirm
       EtherRAT persistence chain. If both fire on the same host within
       minutes, treat as confirmed EtherRAT execution.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104016" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\conhost\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)--headless</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\svchost\.exe$|\\services\.exe$|\\wsl\.exe$|\\wslhost\.exe$)</field>
    <description>EtherRAT: conhost.exe launched with  - headless flag [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] by parent $(win.eventdata.parentImage) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  EtherRAT persistence proxies node.exe execution through conhost  - headless to suppress console window and mask RAT in process trees; non-system conhost  - headless is a defense evasion IOC (T1059.006, T1036.005)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.006</id>
      <id>T1036.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>etherrat,nodejs_rat,conhost_headless,defense_evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104017: HKCU Run EdgeUpdate persistence with non-Edge path
       Sysmon Event 13 (RegistryValueSet) - Wazuh SID 61645

       EtherRAT establishes user-level persistence by writing to the
       Windows Run key under the name "EdgeUpdate":
         HKCU\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run\EdgeUpdate

       The registry value data points to the EtherRAT launcher
       (typically node.exe in the derived %LOCALAPPDATA% directory),
       masquerading as the legitimate Microsoft Edge auto-updater.

       The legitimate Microsoft Edge auto-updater writes to the same
       registry value name but uses a path in Program Files:
         C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft\EdgeUpdate\MicrosoftEdgeUpdate.exe /c
         C:\Program Files\Microsoft\EdgeUpdate\MicrosoftEdgeUpdate.exe /c

       Any write to Run\EdgeUpdate whose value data does NOT reference
       a Program Files EdgeUpdate path is a confirmed EtherRAT IOC.
       This is a high-confidence, low false-positive rule.

       Sysmon registry monitoring must include the Run key path for
       EventID 13 to fire. The targetObject field will contain the
       full key path; the details field contains the new value data.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104017" level="14">
    <if_sid>61645</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^13$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\\EdgeUpdate</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.details" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)C:\\Program Files.*\\Microsoft.*Edge.*\.exe</field>
    <description>EtherRAT: HKCU Run\EdgeUpdate set to non-Edge path [$(win.eventdata.details)] by $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  EtherRAT persistence masquerades as Microsoft Edge auto-updater in HKCU\Run\EdgeUpdate; any value not referencing Program Files EdgeUpdate path is a confirmed EtherRAT IOC; initiate host isolation and incident response (T1547.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>etherrat,nodejs_rat,registry_persistence,ioc_match,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104018: Quick Assist / remote access tool spawning shell
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603

       EtherRAT vishing attacks instruct Teams call victims to share
       screen and grant remote control using one of:
         - Microsoft Quick Assist (QuickAssist.exe)
         - Microsoft Remote Assistance (msra.exe)
         - HopToDesk (HopToDesk.exe)
         - AnyDesk (AnyDesk.exe)

       Under legitimate IT support scenarios, these tools provide
       screen viewing and pointer control. Spawning interactive shells
       (cmd.exe, powershell.exe), script engines (wscript.exe,
       cscript.exe, mshta.exe), node.exe, or msiexec.exe from within
       a remote access session indicates active attacker execution:
       the threat actor is using the remote access channel to run
       the EtherRAT installer or other malicious commands.

       This is a triage/context rule (level 11). It fires on behavior
       that is suspicious but not exclusively malicious (IT admins
       legitimately run scripts via Quick Assist). Correlate with
       Teams call logs, a preceding vishing report, or subsequent
       EtherRAT IOC rules (104015, 104016, 104017) for confirmation.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104018" level="11">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\QuickAssist\.exe$|\\msra\.exe$|\\HopToDesk\.exe$|\\AnyDesk\.exe$)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\cmd\.exe$|\\powershell\.exe$|\\wscript\.exe$|\\cscript\.exe$|\\mshta\.exe$|\\msiexec\.exe$|\\node\.exe$|\\curl\.exe$)</field>
    <description>EtherRAT/Vishing: Remote access tool $(win.eventdata.parentImage) spawned $(win.eventdata.image) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  EtherRAT Teams vishing instructs victims to install Quick Assist or similar RMM tools; shell/installer processes spawned by remote tools indicate active attacker session; correlate with Teams call records and rules 104015-104017 (T1219)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1219</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>etherrat,teams_vishing,rmm_abuse,suspicious_child_process,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104019: EtherRAT svchost.log debug artifact in APPDATA
       Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644

       When debug logging is enabled (and as a default artifact in
       observed EtherRAT variants), the RAT writes a debug log to:
         C:\Users\{user}\AppData\Roaming\svchost.log

       This path is a deliberate masquerade (T1036.005): the name
       "svchost" mimics the Windows Service Host process (svchost.exe)
       to make the log appear as a Windows system artifact. However:
         1. svchost.exe is a SYSTEM-level Windows process that writes
            to %SystemRoot%\System32\ or the Windows Event Log, never
            to a user's APPDATA\Roaming directory.
         2. No legitimate Windows component or common application
            creates a file named "svchost.log" in APPDATA\Roaming.

       Any creation of APPDATA\Roaming\svchost.log is a high-confidence
       EtherRAT IOC. Sysmon FileCreate (EventID 11) must be configured
       to log .log file creation in APPDATA paths for this rule to fire.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104019" level="12">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^11$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)C:\\Users\\[^\\]+\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost\.log$</field>
    <description>EtherRAT: svchost.log debug artifact created at $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) by $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  EtherRAT writes debug logs to %APPDATA%\Roaming\svchost.log masquerading as a Windows service artifact; this file has no legitimate origin in user APPDATA\Roaming; confirmed EtherRAT IOC  -  isolate host and begin forensic triage (T1059.006, T1036.005)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.006</id>
      <id>T1036.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>etherrat,nodejs_rat,debug_artifact,ioc_match,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
